To help you decide, let’s compare the expected values of the two routes in terms of travel time. The highway is always 25 minutes, so that’s a fixed value. The back road, on the other hand, can be either 20 minutes or 40 minutes. We can calculate the expected value of the back road by taking the average of these two times. To do that, we add 20 and 40, then divide by 2.

\begin{align*}
\text{Expected value}&amp;=\text{value}_1+\frac{(\text{value}_2-\text{value}_1)}{2}\\
&amp;=20+\frac{(40-20)}{2}\\
&amp;=20+\frac{20}{2}\\
&amp;=20+10\\
&amp;=\boxed{30}
\end{align*}
The expected value of the back road is 30 minutes. Since this is longer than the 25 minutes of the highway, you should stick with the highway for your commute.

